<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Philistine Pentapolis</h1><p class="st-meta">1175 BCE – 604 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Philistine Pentapolis, five city-states of the southern Levant coast founded by Aegean newcomers, whose name lives on in Palestine.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1175 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom</a> repelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Peleset</a>. Groups of these newcomers settled in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gaza">Gaza</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ashkelon">Ashkelon</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ashdod">Ashdod</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ekron">Ekron</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gath">Gath</a>, bringing Aegean-style pottery, cooking habits and pork-eating to the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each city was ruled by its own lord, called seren in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Bible</a>, and they acted together in war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a> adopted the local Semitic language and gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagon</a>, and became Israel's main rivals in the stories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samson">Samson</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 8th century BCE the cities were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ashkelon">Ashkelon</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ekron">Ekron</a> in 604 BCE and deported their people, ending a separate Philistine identity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called the region Palaestina after them.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/philistine-pentapolis.jpg" alt="Philistine Pentapolis" width="320" height="382"><figcaption>Image: FinnWikiNo; derivative work by Richardprins... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdoms_around_Israel_830_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1175 BCE – 604 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Philistine religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Philistine, Canaanite</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>, <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Philistia, Philistines</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-890&amp;focus=philistine-pentapolis" aria-label="Philistine Pentapolis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5587 1058 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Philistine Pentapolis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5889 1286h0M5891 1281h0M5893 1276h0M5898 1277h0M5898 1280h0M6140 1251h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5907 1314h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5907 1314h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Israel. <a href="/atlas?year=-890&amp;focus=philistine-pentapolis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

